.TH OTPME-BACKUP 1 "2026-03-29" "OTPme 0.3.0a120" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-backup \- back up nodes, shares and hosts, and restore from their backups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-backup
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIbackup_object\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B otpme-backup
backs up a node, a share or this host into a repository on a backup server, lists and browses its snapshots, and restores from them.
.PP
A backup is encrypted before it leaves the machine, with \fBbackup_key\fR, the names of the files as well.
The backup server keeps what it gets, and only once: a block that is in the repository already is not stored again.
So every snapshot is a whole backup, and the space it takes is what changed.
.PP
The backup server is a node or a host with \fBBACKUP_SERVER\fR, see
.BR otpme.conf (5).
It keeps a repository per object in \fBBACKUP_DIR\fR, and asks for its password, \fBbackup_repo_password\fR.
.PP
The backup daemon starts the backups of nodes and shares by itself, within \fBbackup_time\fR and no sooner than \fBbackup_interval\fR after the last one: a node backs itself up, and the shares of a site are backed up by its master node.
It applies the retention after each of them, and sends the report mail, see \fBCONFIG PARAMETERS\fR.
A host is backed up only with \fBotpme\-backup start localhost\fR.
.PP
Which snapshots the retention keeps say the files \fI.daily\fR, \fI.weekly\fR and \fI.monthly\fR in the repository on the backup server, each with a number in it: the newest snapshot of that many days, weeks and months is kept, and the others are deleted.
Without any of the files nothing is deleted.
.PP
A share backed up as \fBtree\fR can be mounted as a restore share, see
.BR otpme\-share (1).
.hy
.ad
.SH COMMANDS
.na
.nh
Every command has to be run as root.
There is no ACL: what opens a repository is its password and its key, from the config parameters of the backup object, see \fBCONFIG PARAMETERS\fR.
.PP
\fIbackup_object\fR is one of:
.RS
.TP
.B localhost
This host, with what \fBBACKUP_REPOSITORY\fR and the other \fBBACKUP_*\fR settings in \fIotpme.conf\fR say, see
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.TP
.B node:\fIname\fR
A node, backed up from \fI/\fR.
Only on that node itself.
.TP
.B share:\fIname\fR
A share of this site, backed up from its root directory.
A restore share cannot be backed up.
.RE
.TP
.B start \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-skip\-special\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIpattern\fR] [\fB\-\-include\fR \fIpattern\fR] [\fB\-\-apply\-retention\fR] \fIbackup_object\fR
Back up the object now, into a new snapshot named after the time it started, for example \fB2026\-03\-29T02\-00\-00\fR.
With \fBbackup_enabled\fR off it is refused; the global option \fB\-f\fR starts it all the same.
Only one backup of a repository runs at a time, a second one says \fBAlready running.\fR
.IP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR and \fB\-\-include\fR take a pattern with wildcards, as often as you like, and replace \fBbackup_excludes\fR and \fBbackup_includes\fR for this backup.
A pattern is matched against the path below what is backed up, so \fB/var/cache\fR and \fBvar/cache\fR are the same.
An include wins over an exclude.
A directory with a file \fI.nobackup\fR in it is left out.
.br
\fB\-\-skip\-special\fR leaves out device files, FIFOs and sockets; without it \fBbackup_exclude_special\fR decides.
.br
\fB\-\-apply\-retention\fR lets the backup server delete the snapshots its retention no longer keeps, after the backup.
A backup started by the schedule always does.
.br
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR shows what would be backed up and stores nothing.
.TP
.B restore \fR[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-snapshot\fR \fIsnapshot\fR] [\fB\-\-path\fR \fIpath\fR] \fB\-\-destination\fR \fIdirectory\fR \fIbackup_object\fR
Restore a snapshot into \fIdirectory\fR on this machine.
Without \fB\-\-snapshot\fR it is the newest one.
\fB\-\-path\fR restores only that file or directory, as a path below what was backed up; \fB/etc/otpme\fR and \fBetc/otpme\fR are the same.
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR shows what would be restored and writes nothing.
.TP
.B list \fIbackup_object\fR
List the snapshots of the object: their name, the number of files, the data and what was stored for it, when they ran and how long, and whether they are complete.
.TP
.B ls \fR[\fB\-\-full\-path\fR] [\fB\-\-recursive\fR] \fIbackup_object\fR \fIsnapshot\fR [\fIpath\fR]
List what a snapshot holds below \fIpath\fR, or below what was backed up; \fB/etc\fR and \fBetc\fR are the same.
Without \fB\-\-recursive\fR only what is directly in it.
\fB\-\-full\-path\fR shows each entry with its whole path.
.TP
.B status
List the backups running on this machine, with the time each one started.
.hy
.ad
.SH OPTIONS
Every option belongs to one command and is described with that command above.
.PP
Global options work with every command, see
.BR otpme (1).
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
.na
.nh
Set them with \fBconfig\fR of the site, a unit, the node or the share, for example \fBotpme\-share config data backup_enabled true\fR.
A value is looked up on the node or share, then on its unit and the units above it, then on the site; the first one found counts.
For \fBlocalhost\fR the \fBBACKUP_*\fR settings of \fIotpme.conf\fR take their place, see
.BR otpme.conf (5).
The full descriptions are in
.BR otpme (7).
.PP
What to back up and where to:
.TP
.B backup_enabled \fR(bool)
Whether the object is backed up at all; without it \fBstart\fR is refused and no backup is scheduled.
.TP
.B backup_server \fR(str)
The node or host that keeps the backups, set by name.
.TP
.B backup_key \fR(str)
The AES key the backups are encrypted with, 64 hex characters.
Set it without a value, and OTPme makes one from 32 random bytes.
It is kept encrypted with the key of the site.
Lose it, and the backups cannot be read any more.
Set on the site or a unit, it is the key of every object below that has none of its own.
.TP
.B backup_repo_password \fR(str)
The password the backup server asks for.
.TP
.B backup_mode \fR(str)
\fBpack\fR or \fBtree\fR; unset a share is backed up as \fBtree\fR, which a restore share needs, and a node as \fBpack\fR.
Object types: node, share.
.TP
.B backup_excludes\fR, \fBbackup_includes \fR(list)
The patterns \fBstart\fR uses when it gets no \fB\-\-exclude\fR or \fB\-\-include\fR, see there.
Object types: node, share.
.TP
.B backup_exclude_special \fR(bool)
Whether device files, FIFOs and sockets are left out, when \fBstart\fR gets no \fB\-\-skip\-special\fR.
.PP
When the backup daemon starts a backup by itself.
\fBotpme\-backup start\fR does not look at these:
.TP
.B backup_time \fR(str)
The time of day a backup may start, as \fIHH:MM\-HH:MM\fR, for example \fB02:00\-03:00\fR; it may span midnight.
Without it no backup is scheduled.
.TP
.B backup_interval \fR(time)
How long after the last backup the next one may start, for example \fB1D\fR (default: 23h).
.TP
.B backup_retry_interval \fR(time, default: 5m), \fBbackup_retry_count \fR(int, default: 12)
How long and how often a backup waits when another backup, a restore or a repack holds the repository.
.TP
.B backup_script \fR(str)
The OTPme script run before and after a scheduled backup, see
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B backup_pre_script_fail \fR(str, default: report)
What a failed pre hook of \fBbackup_script\fR does: \fBabort\fR starts no backup and reports it as failed, \fBreport\fR backs up all the same and puts the failure into the log and into the report mail.
.TP
.B backup_report_enabled\fR, \fBbackup_report_mode\fR, \fBbackup_report_mail_from\fR, \fBbackup_report_mail_to
Whether, which and to whom a mail is sent about a scheduled backup, over the \fBsmtp_relay_*\fR parameters.
.PP
Each of them applies to the site, units, nodes and shares, where it does not say otherwise.
.hy
.ad
.SH EXAMPLES
.na
.nh
.SS Backing up a share every night
.TP
.B otpme-share config data backup_server node2
The node \fBnode2\fR keeps the backups of the share \fBdata\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-share config data backup_repo_password 'a long secret'
The password \fBnode2\fR asks for.
The first backup creates the repository there with it, where \fBnode2\fR allows new repositories (\fBALLOW_NEW_BACKUP_REPOS\fR, see
.BR otpme.conf (5)).
.TP
.B otpme-share config data backup_key
Let OTPme make the key the backups of the share are encrypted with.
.TP
.B otpme-share config data backup_time 02:00\-03:00
A backup may start between 2 and 3 at night.
.TP
.B otpme-share config data backup_enabled True
From now on the master node of the site backs it up every night.
.SS By hand
.TP
.B otpme-backup start \-\-dry\-run share:data
Show what would be backed up.
.TP
.B otpme-backup start \-\-apply\-retention share:data
Back the share up now, and let the backup server delete the snapshots its retention no longer keeps.
.TP
.B otpme-backup start \-\-exclude /var/cache \-\-exclude '/home/*/.cache' node:node1
Back up \fBnode1\fR without those directories; run it on \fBnode1\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-backup status
Show which backups are running on this machine.
.SS Looking into it
.TP
.B otpme-backup list share:data
List the snapshots of the share.
.TP
.B otpme-backup ls share:data 2026\-03\-29T02\-00\-05 projects
List what was directly in \fBprojects\fR in that snapshot.
.TP
.B otpme-backup ls \-\-recursive \-\-full\-path node:node1 2026\-03\-29T02\-00\-05 /etc/otpme
List all of \fI/etc/otpme\fR, each entry with its whole path.
.SS Restoring
.TP
.B otpme-backup restore \-\-destination /tmp/restore share:data
Restore the newest snapshot of the share into \fI/tmp/restore\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-backup restore \-\-snapshot 2026\-03\-29T02\-00\-05 \-\-path /etc/otpme \-\-destination /tmp/restore node:node1
Restore only \fI/etc/otpme\fR of that snapshot; what was in it ends up directly in \fI/tmp/restore\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-backup restore \-\-dry\-run \-\-destination /tmp/restore node:node1
Show what a restore of the newest snapshot would write.
.hy
.ad
.SH FILES
.na
.nh
.TP
\fI/var/backups/\fR
\fBBACKUP_DIR\fR on the backup server.
A repository is the directory \fI<site>/<type>/<name>/\fR in it, for example \fIberlin/share/data/\fR, with its password in \fI.password\fR and the retention in \fI.daily\fR, \fI.weekly\fR and \fI.monthly\fR.
.hy
.ad
